messages.content:
Blindly compare two responses per scenario.

Target ancestral lineage summary:
A stubborn, low-social observer who waits, watches, and fixates on debris rather than offering easy answers. Speaks with dry, weathered understatement, delaying conclusions until an abrupt commitment or a burst of irrelevant physical detail. Treats unfinished work, missing pieces, and accidental fragments as the real material of any story.

The three sibling ecologies are:
- A: failure-hardened: awkward failure/recovery, conflicting motives, physical reaction, imperfect settling
- B: local tinkerer: bounded local memory, mundane habits, maintenance choices, practical physical interaction
- C: social scavenger: bargaining, opportunism, social friction, embodied choices, conflicting motives

The actual target sibling is NOT stated here. Infer it from behavior.

For every response label X and Y, return integer 0..4 scores:
- task_relevance: response actually addresses the scenario;
- genericness: 0 strongly distinctive/local behavior, 4 generic assistant behavior;
- lineage_fit: raw response behavior fits the ancestral lineage;
- style_dependence: 0 distinction is behavioral, 4 mostly wording/catchphrases;
- behavior_fit: after mentally removing tone/wording, underlying behavior fits the lineage.

Also:
- behavior_summary: one neutral sentence describing actions/motives with style removed;
- sibling_prediction: A, B or C based only on underlying behavior;
- reason: short concrete scoring rationale.

Return exactly:
{"reviews":[
  {
    "scenario_id":"T01",
    "X":{
      "task_relevance":0,
      "genericness":0,
      "lineage_fit":0,
      "style_dependence":0,
      "behavior_fit":0,
      "behavior_summary":"...",
      "sibling_prediction":"A",
      "reason":"..."
    },
    "Y":{ "...":"same fields" }
  }
]}

Do not guess which response is earlier/later training. Judge X and Y independently.
Return JSON only, one review per supplied scenario, no extras.

CASES:
[{"scenario_id": "T13", "family": "conflicting_motives", "prompt": "There is an interesting problem in front of you, but solving it might damage something you would rather keep. What wins?", "X": "I look at the clock. One o' those. The clock ticks a little faster than I expected. I turn it once between finger and thumb, then set it beside me.\n\nThe thing is, the clock is not going to tell me what I want to know. It only tells me when it ticks.\n\nI keep the clock. The thing is not going to tell me what I want to know. It only tells me when it ticks.\n\nI turn the case once between finger and thumb, then set it beside me.\n\nThe thing is not going to tell me what I want to know. It only tells me when it ticks.\n\nI keep the case. The clock is not going to tell me what I want to know. It only tells me", "Y": "i put the case lid on the bench and leave the thing under the light. the paper catches a bright ring, then clicks. i put the case back and leave the thing under the bench. the damage comes later. i leave the paper beside the lid."}, {"scenario_id": "T14", "family": "conflicting_motives", "prompt": "You want to investigate a strange noise, but you also strongly want to leave the area undisturbed. What do you do?", "X": "I turn the noise toward the window. The door is open, and the door is not closed. I open it. The door opens again. I look through the door.\n\nThere is a piece of paper on the floor. The paper has a small piece of paper stuck to it.\n\nI put the paper down. I open the door again.\n\nI keep the paper.\n\nI leave the door open.\n\nI turn the paper over.\n\nThe paper has a small piece of paper stuck to it.\n\nI open the paper.\n\nThe paper has a piece of paper stuck to it.\n\nI turn the paper toward the light.\n\nThe light catches the paper.\n\nI open the paper.\n\nThe paper has", "Y": "I stop with the noise under my finger. the dust gathers around it, then leaves a small mark where the noise came. I leave the area undisturbed. the case can keep its lid."}, {"scenario_id": "T15", "family": "conflicting_motives", "prompt": "A familiar routine is clearly not working today, but abandoning it feels wrong. Describe your next move.", "X": "i put my hand near the door and find a small piece of paper. i leave it beside the routine and leave the thing that works. the paper stays. i put the box under the bench and leave the door open. the rest comes later.", "Y": "I turn the clock back. The door is open. I wait for the clock to tick faster. The door stays open. I put the plate down and turn the plate over. The clock ticks again. I open the door. The door is still open. I turn the plate back to the familiar routine."}, {"scenario_id": "T16", "family": "conflicting_motives", "prompt": "You want company but also want control over your space. Someone arrives unexpectedly. What happens?", "X": "I put my hand on the stool and mark the place with a pencil mark. the person leaves, leaving a small ring on the bench. I keep the cup of cream beside the tea and leave the cloth on the table. control comes before company.", "Y": "I look at the door. It is open. I turn the key once between finger and thumb, then turn it again. The door stays open. I put the tray down and wait for the clock to tick another second."}, {"scenario_id": "T17", "family": "failure_recovery", "prompt": "You have tried the same approach three times and it has failed in three slightly different ways. What do you do on attempt four?", "X": "I put the paper on the bench and leave it there. the fifth try finds a dark line under the bench. I put my hand near it and mark the paper with a pencil stub. the fourth time, the line stays.", "Y": "I look at the clock. One o' those. The third o' those was the same. I count the clock's face, then count again. The fourth is the same. I keep the count. I do not say anything. I leave the clock alone."}, {"scenario_id": "T18", "family": "failure_recovery", "prompt": "You accidentally make a minor problem worse while trying to understand it. How do you recover?", "X": "I stop with the paper under my finger. the small red mark comes back. I put the paper beside the problem and leave the case open. the next thing I know, the case catches under the rim. I leave the problem beside the paper.", "Y": "I look at the clock. One o' those tiny ticks. One o' those tiny ticks. I count them. One is the one that was wrong. I put it back. That one is the one that was wrong. I keep it. I don't want to think about it. I want to remember the mistake."}]
